WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:03.240
Speaker 1: Let's go next.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.359
Why don't we go to Mike from Massachusetts?

00:00:06.359 --> 00:00:07.420
Mike, welcome.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:10.620
Speaker 4: It is my pleasure.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:13.500
I have a couple quick questions.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:18.029
I you want to get to as many people as possible,
but I'll try to make them quick.

00:00:18.029 --> 00:00:22.850
So while there's obviously still some of the
races out there that still need to be called

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:24.670
the important ones.

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:32.060
Clearly, this was not the red wave that the
race was, you know, drooling about, you know,

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:35.700
to totally dominate Congress and, you know,
block everything for Joe Biden.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:41.010
What do you think that I mean, what do you
think the Democratic establishment is going

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.980
to take away from this?

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:48.219
Because one thing that people have pointed
out is that the only Senate seat that so far

00:00:48.219 --> 00:00:53.640
they've been able to flip was Fetterman, who
ran on a relatively progressive agenda.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:58.030
Do you think they take anything away from
this to take it in 2024, or do they just continue

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:00.550
to keep the status quo because that's just
what they like to do?

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:05.820
Speaker 1: And fortunately or unfortunately,
depending on your perspective, I would say,

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:08.530
unfortunately, this was such a unique midterm.

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:13.420
And you're right, there's races where votes
are still being counted that I don't think

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:16.189
there is one big takeaway.

00:01:16.189 --> 00:01:22.930
And the truth is, I've said for a long time,
different types of Democrats make sense in

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:25.750
different parts of the country.

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:30.829
Would it be great to be able to run progressives
in every district, every Senate race and every

00:01:30.829 --> 00:01:33.270
governor's race and win?

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:34.270
Sure.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:37.159
But that just simply doesn't work that way.

00:01:37.159 --> 00:01:45.380
You know, up in Massachusetts, the governor's
race, Maura Healey, not like the uber progressive.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:51.920
And when you understand, well, Massachusetts
has a history of more centrist Democrats and

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:58.860
Republican governors, you have to kind of
say, well, if we if we ran like a super progressive,

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:02.130
it might end up with another Republican governor
in Massachusetts.

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:03.500
I'm sure you understand that.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:04.930
And so I honestly.

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:05.930
Speaker 4: I'm sorry.

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:06.930
Sorry.

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:10.610
I was going to say in this particular case
from Massachusetts, I mean, the Republican

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:16.300
Party went from a very moderate like Baker
and leaped all the way to deal a right wing

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:17.300
nut like Neil.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:18.300
So.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:19.300
Deal.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:20.300
Yeah, I mean, deal.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:21.300
Sorry.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:22.300
Yeah.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.650
So, I mean, I don't, I think it was always
a foregone conclusion.

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:26.390
Maura Healey And that's one of the bad, yes.

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:30.209
Speaker 1: No, it maybe it's not the best
example this particular year, but it is sort

00:02:30.209 --> 00:02:31.209
of an example.

00:02:31.209 --> 00:02:37.960
So I think the takeaway from it for Democrats
really needs to be different.

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:44.310
Different candidates in different races can
make sense, don't compromise on what are the

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.880
most important uniting values.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:50.080
And I would say one thing about Fetterman,
you know, I don't there was this confusing

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:51.080
moment about fracking.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:53.950
At the end of the day, Fetterman said he's
for fracking.

00:02:53.950 --> 00:03:00.420
So it he sort of backed off of as progressive
a campaign as he had been running.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:03.390
And I know that there was some confusion during
the debate about that, but he was like, no,

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:04.390
no, no, I'm for fracking.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:05.470
I'm for fracking.

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:08.830
So did he really run as progressively as maybe
you're characterizing?

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:09.830
I don't know.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:12.010
But listen, it's a great thing that Fetterman
won.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:13.370
Speaker 4: That's fair.

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:15.060
And I agree with that.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:18.299
One more if you'll allow me and I'll let you
move on.

00:03:18.299 --> 00:03:23.000
This one's probably going to get a few eyeroll
some from listeners, but I, I've just been

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.860
really thinking about this and maybe the perspective
has changed since this election didn't go

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:28.080
as we thought it might.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:34.799
But I mean, do you feel like as a country
we're approaching a position where.

00:03:34.799 --> 00:03:38.580
You know, just based on the division that
we have, the irreconcilable differences.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:42.810
I mean, do you think we're approaching a point
where we have to have a legitimate conversation

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:48.180
about doing do is it the best is it in everyone's
best interest to keep the country together?

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:51.280
I mean, do you think we're approaching a point
where we have to have a legitimate conversation

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:53.489
about breaking the country up into two or
more pieces?

00:03:53.489 --> 00:03:55.060
Speaker 1: I don't.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:04:00.519
And the reason why, honestly, is exactly as
you said, two or more.

00:04:00.519 --> 00:04:06.269
There is a lack of contiguity in exactly how
you would do it, even if we agreed we've got

00:04:06.269 --> 00:04:07.269
to do it.

00:04:07.269 --> 00:04:12.829
And it makes sense, which I don't believe
anywhere near a plurality of people are at.

00:04:12.829 --> 00:04:16.959
I actually don't know geographically that
there's any you would end up needing like

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:18.340
six countries.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:20.680
And obviously that's not going to happen,
you know?

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:21.739
Speaker 4: Fair.

00:04:21.739 --> 00:04:26.350
It's just hard to it's hard to imagine how
we keep moving forward in a positive direction.

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:27.790
Speaker 1: How do we live with these people?

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:31.780
I mean, it's an alternate reality, an alternate
set of moral values.

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:36.590
It's no bar is too low for any candidate no
matter what, as long as they're not a Democrat.

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:38.580
Herschel Walker being the perfect example.

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:39.580
It's crazy.

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:41.150
I share the desperation.

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:44.740
I just don't think chopping the country up
into two or more pieces is going to be a thing

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:45.970
in the next few years.

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:48.870
Speaker 4: I know, and I don't know if that
would be the best move, because that would

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:52.280
remove a serious roadblock for countries like
China and Russia.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:54.370
Speaker 1: Absolutely.

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:56.550
Never mind the chaos it would wreak domestically.

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:58.190
Speaker 4: Fair.

00:04:58.190 --> 00:05:01.919
Fair is just it is hard to imagine how we
keep moving forward with such drastic differences.

00:05:01.919 --> 00:05:02.919
I'm waiting.

00:05:02.919 --> 00:05:03.919
Speaker 1: I'm with you.

00:05:03.919 --> 00:05:05.050
Speaker 4: All right.

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:06.470
Thank you for your time.

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:07.610
Speaker 1: All right.

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:09.080
Thanks, Mike from Massachusetts.

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:09.990
Great to hear from you.

